WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.091
Container patch management is primarily a rebuild, test, redeploy, and verify discipline.

00:00:07.091 --> 00:00:22.061
Teams update controlled inputs rather than manually editing running containers, producing immutable image digests that can be traced from source and base image to the deployed workload.

00:00:22.061 --> 00:00:31.778
Effective discovery combines image scanning, software composition analysis, package inventories, SBOMs, current vulnerability data, and runtime mapping.

00:00:31.778 --> 00:00:39.920
Findings require validation because scanner coverage, package metadata, and actual deployment all affect accuracy.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:49.112
Prioritization combines severity with exploitability, known exploitation, exposure, workload criticality, compensating controls, environment, and base-image fan-out.

00:00:49.112 --> 00:00:57.779
Remediation then moves through automated tests, integration and staging, controlled rollout, monitoring, and rollback planning.

00:00:57.779 --> 00:01:10.123
CI/CD automation can propose updates, scan artifacts, enforce policy gates, record provenance, sign images, protect registries, and promote the same reviewed digest.

00:01:10.123 --> 00:01:16.688
Automation should accelerate decisions without removing review, ownership, or exception governance.

00:01:16.688 --> 00:01:24.305
Nodes, runtimes, Kubernetes components, and cluster add-ons need separate maintenance from application images.

00:01:24.305 --> 00:01:35.073
Strong governance assigns owners, defines risk-based SLAs, manages expiring exceptions, and measures verified deployment rather than fixed artifacts alone.

00:01:35.073 --> 00:01:41.638
The goal is secure, reliable, repeatable remediation without unnecessary production disruption.
